1
00:00:38,056 --> 00:00:43,051
I was on the receiving end of some frightful pressure from the American
ambassador at that drinkies do last night.

2
00:00:43,217 --> 00:00:46,606
- Ah.
- Apparently, they'd heard about my cancelling Trident.

3
00:00:46,776 --> 00:00:48,369
Ah.

4
00:00:48,536 --> 00:00:52,529
It seems that the American defence industries
are among the biggest contributors to the President party funds.

5
00:00:52,697 --> 00:00:57,407
- Ah.
- Then he said we had a big problem in East Yemen.

6
00:00:57,576 --> 00:00:59,533
Ah?

7
00:00:59,737 --> 00:01:04,127
Stop saying "ah" like that, Bernard.
I'm not your doctor.

8
00:01:05,457 --> 00:01:08,334
- I'm sorry Prime Minister.
- What is this big problem in East Yemen?

9
00:01:08,535 --> 00:01:10,492
Ah...

10
00:01:10,696 --> 00:01:13,689
- Thank you, Bernard.
- Well.. I'll try and find out.

11
00:01:13,856 --> 00:01:18,133
- Then he muttered something about St George's Island.
- St George's Island?

12
00:01:18,335 --> 00:01:23,285
- Yes. There IS a St George's Island? Isn't it?
- Yes, it's in the Commonwealth.

13
00:01:23,496 --> 00:01:26,294
Ah, see, yes! THAT St George's Island. yes of course!

14
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:31,975
- Anyway apparently, he's afraid that the communists might try to grab it.
- What did you say?

15
00:01:32,177 --> 00:01:35,168
- I said I would have a word with the Foreign Office.
- What did he say?

16
00:01:35,335 --> 00:01:39,046
He sort of gave a hollow laugh. Apparently, the White House thinks
the Foreign Office is full of pinkos and traitors.

17
00:01:39,257 --> 00:01:42,248
No, it's not. Well, not full.

18
00:01:42,416 --> 00:01:47,013
They don't want the Reds to get
a strategic base like St George's Island.

19
00:01:47,177 --> 00:01:52,171
He spoke about putting tariffs on British
exports to the USA and taxing US investment to Britain,

20
00:01:52,335 --> 00:01:54,487
causing a run on the pound.

21
00:01:54,696 --> 00:01:59,815
Well you are seeing the Foreign Secretary this afternoon.
Can't you get him to sort it out? After all, they are on our side.

22
00:02:00,016 --> 00:02:02,210
- Who are?
- The Americans.

23
00:02:02,416 --> 00:02:05,214
For a moment, I thought you meant the Foreign Office.

24
00:02:06,216 --> 00:02:11,209
What's the Foreign Office worried about then, Dick?
Haven't you got the Foreign Secretary eating out of your hand.

25
00:02:11,375 --> 00:02:14,731
- Oh, yes. Completely house-trained.
- So what's the problem?

26
00:02:14,936 --> 00:02:20,647
The Prime Minister's the problem.
He's starting to mistrust our advice
when the foreign secretary gives it to him.
- Ah!

27
00:02:20,855 --> 00:02:25,690
- He even beginning to question the Foreign Office policy.
- Surely not!

28
00:02:25,895 --> 00:02:29,525
We even see there is a danger the Cabinet
may pursue its own foreign policy.

29
00:02:29,736 --> 00:02:33,853
But that's absurd. The country
can't have two foreign policies!

30
00:02:34,055 --> 00:02:38,572
Quite. And the PM's getting far too keen
on keeping the White House happy.

31
00:02:38,775 --> 00:02:41,370
Dear, oh, dear, oh dear. So, how can I help?

32
00:02:41,575 --> 00:02:46,365
Well there are a couple of things coming up
where he might need guiding in the right direction.

33
00:02:46,535 --> 00:02:50,528
- One of them is St George's Island.
- Ah. Oh, yes, yes, yes.

34
00:02:50,696 --> 00:02:53,653
Remind me, would you, Dick?

35
00:02:53,855 --> 00:03:00,455
Well, as you know, it's one of the few islands in the Indian Ocean to stay in the Royal Commonwealth after independence.

36
00:03:00,656 --> 00:03:03,613
Oh, yes, that's right.
It's quite democratic.

37
00:03:03,815 --> 00:03:06,205
Yes. Free elections and everything.

38
00:03:06,375 --> 00:03:12,086
But there's a group of Marxist guerrillas in the
mountains somewhere. And we are getting reports they're planning a coup.

39
00:03:12,295 --> 00:03:15,093
Oh well, these things will happen.

40
00:03:15,256 --> 00:03:19,647
They're not thinking of starting it
on their own. They are going to be helped by East Yemen.

41
00:03:19,855 --> 00:03:25,248
- East Yemen - Isn't that a democracy?
- Its full name is People's Democratic Republic of East Yemen.

42
00:03:25,456 --> 00:03:28,050
Oh, I see. It's a Communist dictatorship.

43
00:03:28,216 --> 00:03:31,208
And the guerrillas are Soviet-backed? Are they?

44
00:03:31,416 --> 00:03:36,649
Yes, and Libyan-backed. So obviously, we in the
Foreign Office are planning to stay right out of it.

45
00:03:36,855 --> 00:03:38,733
Obviously.

46
00:03:38,936 --> 00:03:42,689
It would only upset
a whole lot of frontline African states, we got involved with.

47
00:03:42,895 --> 00:03:45,535
And we don't want to
antagonise the Soviets at this time, do we?

48
00:03:45,736 --> 00:03:51,526
Quite. And we've just landed a huge contract to
build a new St George's airport and harbour installation.

49
00:03:51,696 --> 00:03:54,449
If we back the wrong side, we may lose the contract.

50
00:03:54,656 --> 00:03:59,207
Quite. We don't mind which side wins,
the democrats or the Marxists?

51
00:03:59,416 --> 00:04:01,804
Why should we? But you see the problem.

52
00:04:01,976 --> 00:04:06,766
If the PM gets into one of his ghastly patriotic
Churchillian moods, he may want to intervene.

53
00:04:06,936 --> 00:04:10,325
All that pro-British,
defending democracy nonsense.

54
00:04:10,535 --> 00:04:13,447
Oh, I know, I know, I know.

55
00:04:13,656 --> 00:04:18,651
He must understand that once you start interfering
in the internal squabbles of other countries,

56
00:04:18,817 --> 00:04:23,445
you're on a very slippery slope.
Even the Foreign Secretary's grasped that.

57
00:04:24,497 --> 00:04:27,533
Really? So what was the other point?

58
00:04:27,736 --> 00:04:31,935
Yes, well the Arabs have put down a motion
at the United Nations condemning Israel.

59
00:04:32,096 --> 00:04:35,089
- Naturally, we'll vote on the Arab side.
- Naturally.

60
00:04:35,255 --> 00:04:39,488
- But I gather the PM wants us to abstain.
- Surely not! Why?

61
00:04:39,695 --> 00:04:45,488
Something about the PLO starting it this time,
faults on both sides. The usual sentimental nonsense.
- oh!

62
00:04:45,656 --> 00:04:48,091
Sucking up to the Americans, as always.

63
00:04:48,296 --> 00:04:53,087
Just help him to realise that,
as far as foreign affairs are concerned,

64
00:04:53,255 --> 00:04:58,377
his job is to confine himself
to the hospitality and ceremonial role.

65
00:04:58,575 --> 00:05:03,365
I'll do my best, but you know what happens
when politicians get into Number 10.

66
00:05:03,536 --> 00:05:06,334
They want to take their place on the world stage.

67
00:05:06,536 --> 00:05:08,925
People on stages are called actors.

68
00:05:09,096 --> 00:05:14,613
All they're required to do is look plausible,
stay sober and say the lines they're given in the right order.

69
00:05:14,817 --> 00:05:17,454
Some of them try to make
up their own lines.

70
00:05:17,656 --> 00:05:20,296
They, don't last long.

71
00:05:22,136 --> 00:05:26,447
One of the American ambassador
had a word with me last night. Unofficially

72
00:05:26,615 --> 00:05:29,654
- Ah! About what?
- What do you know about St George's Island?

73
00:05:36,055 --> 00:05:38,365
What do YOU know about it?

74
00:05:38,575 --> 00:05:41,171
Well, you're the Foreign Secretary, not me!

75
00:05:42,856 --> 00:05:45,245
Is there any danger of
a Communist takeover?

76
00:05:45,457 --> 00:05:48,447
- Well, did he say there was?
- He hinted.

77
00:05:49,615 --> 00:05:54,769
No. No chance at all. No chance at all.
Of course! The Foreign Office would have told me.

78
00:05:54,976 --> 00:06:00,687
- You're sure the Foreign Office always tell you everything?
- Well, everything they think I should know.

79
00:06:00,897 --> 00:06:06,289
That's what I'm afraid of... anyway. The Americans are
worried. We just can't afford to upset them now.

80
00:06:06,497 --> 00:06:11,729
- I'm sure we've got everything under control.
- Chamberlain thought he had Hitler under control.

81
00:06:11,937 --> 00:06:15,849
- Ah, well, Chamberlain.
- Eden thought he had Nasser under control.

82
00:06:16,055 --> 00:06:20,846
- Are you suggesting that the F.O. doesn't know what it's doing?
- No, I am suggesting they are not telling us what they're doing!

83
00:06:21,937 --> 00:06:25,690
But that's absurd! They give me a full answer
to any questions I ask.

84
00:06:25,897 --> 00:06:28,694
- And the questions you don't ask?
- Such as?

85
00:06:28,856 --> 00:06:33,692
- Such as about St George's Island.
- Ah, well, I don't ask those.

86
00:06:33,897 --> 00:06:37,889
- Well, just ask them. For me. All right?
- Yes, of course. If that's what you want.

87
00:06:38,055 --> 00:06:43,654
But dont forget, once you start interfering in the internal
squabbles of another country, you're on a very slippery slope.

88
00:06:43,817 --> 00:06:45,885
Anything else?

89
00:06:46,096 --> 00:06:51,171
Yes, I gather we're proposing to vote
against Israel in the UN tonight.
- Of course!
- Why?

90
00:06:51,377 --> 00:06:54,971
- They bombed the PLO.
- The PLO bombed Israel.

91
00:06:55,175 --> 00:06:58,533
- But the Israelis dropped more bombs!
- The PLO started it!

92
00:06:58,736 --> 00:07:03,173
- No, they didn't.
- But anyway, it seems to me there are faults on both sides.

93
00:07:03,377 --> 00:07:08,291
- Not according to my advice.
- Well the Americans are worried. They want us to abstain.

94
00:07:08,497 --> 00:07:12,694
I don't think we could do that.
- why not?
The Foreign Office wouldn't wear it.

95
00:07:12,897 --> 00:07:16,685
Are they here to follow our instructions
or are we to follow theirs?

96
00:07:16,897 --> 00:07:19,046
Now don't be silly.

97
00:07:22,656 --> 00:07:25,046
Prime Minister, Sir Humphrey's here.

98
00:07:25,216 --> 00:07:27,810
- Prime Minister.
- Humphrey, I'm worried.

99
00:07:27,976 --> 00:07:30,365
Oh, what about, Prime Minister?

100
00:07:32,055 --> 00:07:36,892
- About the Americans.
- Well, we're all worried about the Americans.

101
00:07:37,096 --> 00:07:42,694
- Foreign affairs are a complicated business.
- That's why we leave it to the Foreign Office.

102
00:07:42,897 --> 00:07:47,253
I've got to keep in with the Americans
if I'm to cancel our defence order.

103
00:07:47,457 --> 00:07:52,325
- Ah.
- I gather that they're worried about us.

104
00:07:52,536 --> 00:07:56,212
Of course they are,
but I know how to keep in with them.

105
00:07:56,416 --> 00:07:59,327
- How?
- Don't cancel the huge defence order.

106
00:07:59,536 --> 00:08:04,132
No, Humphrey, I'm determined on that,
but we mustn't upset them in any other way.

107
00:08:04,296 --> 00:08:07,606
The ambassador mentioned
St George's Island.

108
00:08:08,497 --> 00:08:12,694
- Oh, really?
- What's going on in that part of the world?

109
00:08:13,776 --> 00:08:16,164
What part of the world?

110
00:08:16,377 --> 00:08:20,209
That part. The part where
St George's Island is.

111
00:08:21,216 --> 00:08:23,411
What part is that?

112
00:08:23,615 --> 00:08:27,973
- I suggest you go and have a look at the map!
- No, I do know, Prime Minister.

113
00:08:28,175 --> 00:08:31,486
Good. Then we both know.

114
00:08:33,457 --> 00:08:38,132
The White House is worried
it might be taken over by Marxists.

115
00:08:39,457 --> 00:08:43,450
- It's not funny, Humphrey.
- No, indeed. Rather touching, really.

116
00:08:43,616 --> 00:08:48,611
Prime Minister, once you start interfering in
the internal squabbles of other countries,

117
00:08:48,777 --> 00:08:51,370
you're on a very slippery slope.

118
00:08:51,536 --> 00:08:55,893
- That's what the Foreign Secretary said.
- Extraordinary.

119
00:08:56,096 --> 00:09:01,216
Then there's the U.N. vote on Israel
tonight. The Americans want us to abstain.

120
00:09:01,417 --> 00:09:06,888
It's a question of maintaining our relationship
with the Arabs. The power of Islam.

121
00:09:07,056 --> 00:09:09,446
Oil supplies.

122
00:09:09,656 --> 00:09:12,011
I'm talking about what's right and wrong.

123
00:09:12,216 --> 00:09:14,777
Well, don't let the
Foreign Office hear you.

124
00:09:14,937 --> 00:09:20,330
If you insist on an even-handed approach, the
Foreign Office might agree to abstaining,

125
00:09:20,497 --> 00:09:25,491
so long as you authorise our man there to
make a powerful speech attacking Zionism.

126
00:09:25,696 --> 00:09:28,973
Surely we should promote
peace, harmony, goodwill.

127
00:09:29,176 --> 00:09:31,565
Well, it would be most unusual.

128
00:09:31,736 --> 00:09:36,127
The U.N. is the accepted forum
for the expression of international hatred.

129
00:09:36,337 --> 00:09:42,286
- And defending democracy on St George's?
- Not if it harms us by upsetting our friends.

130
00:09:42,497 --> 00:09:45,533
Britain should not support law and justice?

131
00:09:45,736 --> 00:09:51,573
Of course we should. We just shouldn't let
it affect our foreign policy, that's all.

132
00:09:51,777 --> 00:09:54,165
We must fight for the
weak against the strong.

133
00:09:54,377 --> 00:09:58,495
Then send troops to Afghanistan
to fight the Russians.

134
00:09:58,696 --> 00:10:01,256
The Russians are too strong.

135
00:10:03,656 --> 00:10:06,046
What was that about law and justice?

136
00:10:06,256 --> 00:10:10,647
I want assurances sent
to the Prime Minister of St George's Island

137
00:10:10,817 --> 00:10:13,125
that Britain will stand by him.

138
00:10:13,337 --> 00:10:17,694
- Discuss this with the Foreign Secretary.
- Thank you, Humphrey.

139
00:10:17,897 --> 00:10:22,288
- Thank you, Prime Minister.
- You've been a great help

140
00:10:23,457 --> 00:10:26,051
- Bernard?
- Yes, Prime Minister?

141
00:10:26,216 --> 00:10:28,411
Where exactly is St George's Island?

142
00:10:28,616 --> 00:10:30,413
Um...

143
00:10:31,417 --> 00:10:35,933
- Shall we look at the globe?
- Good idea.

144
00:10:40,656 --> 00:10:44,649
- Good afternoon, Luke.
- Good afternoon, Prime Minister.

145
00:10:44,817 --> 00:10:47,011
Now, where is it?

146
00:10:49,056 --> 00:10:50,853
Eh...

147
00:10:51,017 --> 00:10:55,009
- It's here, in the Arabian Sea.
- Near the Persian Gulf.

148
00:10:55,176 --> 00:10:59,772
The lifeline of the West. There's
Afghanistan, now under Soviet control.

149
00:10:59,937 --> 00:11:02,326
Only 400 miles north of the Arabian Sea.

150
00:11:02,497 --> 00:11:06,489
- If the Soviets ever took Pakistan...
- Which they wouldn't.

151
00:11:06,656 --> 00:11:11,651
If they did, they'd control the Persian
Gulf, the Arabian Sea and the Indian Ocean.

152
00:11:11,817 --> 00:11:15,411
They've always wanted a "warm water port".

153
00:11:15,616 --> 00:11:20,611
They wouldn't invade Pakistan. The Americans
have a fleet permanently stationed here.

154
00:11:20,777 --> 00:11:23,370
The Indian Ocean.

155
00:11:24,656 --> 00:11:29,047
Prime Minister,
may I have a word about... home affairs?

156
00:11:29,216 --> 00:11:32,129
- Home affairs?
- Yes.

157
00:11:32,337 --> 00:11:37,855
- We're discussing foreign affairs.
- No, Prime Minister, it's rather urgent.

158
00:11:38,056 --> 00:11:41,844
- Are you all right, Bernard?
- Yes, Prime Minister. Please.

159
00:11:42,017 --> 00:11:44,406
Very well. Excuse me, Luke.

160
00:11:47,457 --> 00:11:51,051
- What on earth's the matter?
- I don't want to be disloyal,

161
00:11:51,216 --> 00:11:56,210
but it wasn't a good idea to continue that
conversation in front of Luke. Security.

162
00:11:56,417 --> 00:12:01,730
He's your colleague, one of my private
secretaries. What is MI6 up to...?

163
00:12:01,937 --> 00:12:06,134
- Not that sort of risk. He works for the F.O.
- He works for me.

164
00:12:06,337 --> 00:12:11,535
Eh... yes, but he's not only your man
from the Foreign Office,

165
00:12:11,696 --> 00:12:16,134
he's also their man in Number 10.
He's a plant.

166
00:12:16,337 --> 00:12:18,453
Oh. Ah.

167
00:12:18,616 --> 00:12:20,413
Oh.

168
00:12:20,576 --> 00:12:23,774
- Do you understand?
- Yes. No!

169
00:12:25,576 --> 00:12:29,774
- The F.O. is keeping something from me?
- Yes.

170
00:12:29,937 --> 00:12:34,294
- What?
- I don't know. They're keeping it from me, too.

171
00:12:34,497 --> 00:12:36,852
- Then how do you know?
- I don't know.

172
00:12:37,017 --> 00:12:40,406
How do you know if you don't know?!

173
00:12:40,616 --> 00:12:45,611
I don't know specifically what, but I do know
they always keep everything from everybody.

174
00:12:45,777 --> 00:12:48,337
- It's normal practice.
- Who does know?

175
00:12:48,536 --> 00:12:50,926
May I just clarify the question?

176
00:12:51,096 --> 00:12:55,692
You are asking who would know what it is that I
don't know and you don't know but the F.O. know

177
00:12:55,857 --> 00:13:00,647
that they keep from you so you don't know and
all we know is there is something we don't know

178
00:13:00,817 --> 00:13:06,368
and we want to know, but we don't know what
because we don't know? Is that it?

179
00:13:07,056 --> 00:13:12,848
May I clarify? Who knows Foreign Office
secrets apart from the Foreign Office?

180
00:13:13,056 --> 00:13:15,651
Oh, that's easy. Only the Kremlin.

181
00:13:18,857 --> 00:13:22,565
This bother about St George's Island
is getting to be a bore.

182
00:13:22,777 --> 00:13:26,611
We made the real mistake
giving them their independence.

183
00:13:26,817 --> 00:13:29,410
Wasn't that right? Wind of change and all?

184
00:13:29,576 --> 00:13:33,569
Yes, but not that way.
We should have partitioned the island.

185
00:13:33,736 --> 00:13:37,616
Like we did in India, Cyprus and Palestine?
And Ireland?

186
00:13:37,817 --> 00:13:42,844
Yes, that was our invariable practice
with the colonies. It always worked.

187
00:13:44,056 --> 00:13:46,446
But didn't partition
always lead to civil war?

188
00:13:46,616 --> 00:13:51,611
- As in India, Cyprus, Palestine and Ireland.
- Yes, but it kept them busy.

189
00:13:51,777 --> 00:13:56,292
Instead of fighting other people,
they fought each other.

190
00:13:56,497 --> 00:14:00,774
Yes, rather good. Saved us having a policy.
Cheers.

191
00:14:01,777 --> 00:14:04,575
- Sir Humphrey?
- Yes, Bernard?

192
00:14:04,736 --> 00:14:07,535
- Um, Sir Humphrey...
- Yes, Bernard?

193
00:14:07,696 --> 00:14:10,085
- Could I have a word?
- Yes, Bernard?

194
00:14:10,256 --> 00:14:12,645
- With both of you.
- Yes. What about?

195
00:14:12,817 --> 00:14:14,808
- St George's.
- What now?

196
00:14:15,017 --> 00:14:17,770
The PM seems to be completely in the dark.

197
00:14:17,977 --> 00:14:22,413
- Good.
- Excellent. Anything else?

198
00:14:22,616 --> 00:14:25,290
I wonder, is there
anything he doesn't know?

199
00:14:25,497 --> 00:14:27,886
I hardly know where to begin, Bernard.

200
00:14:29,777 --> 00:14:34,292
- No, I mean anything important.
- He has the Foreign Secretary to tell him.

201
00:14:34,497 --> 00:14:38,888
- He seems to think HE doesn't know either.
- I should hope not!

202
00:14:40,056 --> 00:14:45,415
- Are you implying that he ought to know?
- Well, he IS the Prime Minister.

203
00:14:45,616 --> 00:14:50,770
Bernard, it's simply too dangerous to let
politicians become involved with diplomacy.

204
00:14:50,977 --> 00:14:56,448
Diplomacy is about surviving until the next
century. Politics is about surviving to Friday!

205
00:14:56,656 --> 00:15:01,651
There are 157 independent countries in the
world. We've dealt with them for years.

206
00:15:01,817 --> 00:15:04,728
There's hardly an MP who knows anything.

207
00:15:04,897 --> 00:15:09,732
Show them a map of the world,
most have a job finding the Isle of Wight.

208
00:15:09,937 --> 00:15:12,928
Surely politicians can't be that ignorant?

209
00:15:14,056 --> 00:15:16,446
Very well. Sit down, Bernard.

210
00:15:16,616 --> 00:15:19,609
- Where is the Upper Volta?
- Em...

211
00:15:19,777 --> 00:15:22,768
- What's the capital of Chad?
- Um...

212
00:15:22,937 --> 00:15:27,408
- What language do they speak in Mali?
- Em...

213
00:15:27,616 --> 00:15:31,326
- Who's the president of Peru?
- Um...

214
00:15:31,536 --> 00:15:34,131
What is the national religion of Cameroon?

215
00:15:35,337 --> 00:15:38,373
- Em...
- Bernard?

216
00:15:38,576 --> 00:15:40,966
You should stand for Parliament.

217
00:15:42,377 --> 00:15:47,292
No, what I mean is if it's a democracy,
shouldn't people sort of discuss things a bit?

218
00:15:47,497 --> 00:15:50,408
- Of course. Full discussion.
- And have the facts?

219
00:15:50,616 --> 00:15:55,007
Of course not! They don't want them.
Facts complicate things.

220
00:15:55,176 --> 00:16:00,569
All the press, the people and their leaders want
to know is who are the goodies and baddies.

221
00:16:00,777 --> 00:16:06,056
The interests of Britain nearly always involve
doing deals with people they think are baddies.

222
00:16:06,256 --> 00:16:09,134
And not helping the goodies occasionally.

223
00:16:09,337 --> 00:16:11,931
So we avoid discussion of foreign affairs.

224
00:16:12,096 --> 00:16:14,486
Or rather we keep it
inside the Foreign Office

225
00:16:14,656 --> 00:16:20,288
then we produce one policy for the Foreign
Secretary, which is our considered view.

226
00:16:20,497 --> 00:16:24,011
- No options? No alternatives?
- None.

227
00:16:24,216 --> 00:16:28,495
- What if he's not satisfied?
- If pressed, we look at it again.

228
00:16:28,696 --> 00:16:32,895
- And come up with a different view?
- Of course not! The same view.

229
00:16:33,096 --> 00:16:36,089
- What if he demands options?
- It's obvious.

230
00:16:36,256 --> 00:16:42,127
The F.O. present him with three options, two
of which are, in fact, exactly the same.

231
00:16:42,337 --> 00:16:44,531
And the third is totally unacceptable.

232
00:16:44,736 --> 00:16:47,410
Like bombing Warsaw or invading France.

233
00:16:47,616 --> 00:16:52,406
We occasionally encourage the Foreign
Secretary to produce his own policy,

234
00:16:52,576 --> 00:16:58,334
then we tell him it will inevitably lead to
World War Three, perhaps within 48 hours.

235
00:16:58,536 --> 00:17:03,690
- I see. I'm sorry to appear stupid...
- Oh, perish the thought, Bernard.

236
00:17:05,457 --> 00:17:10,655
In my experience, ministers are concerned about
the effect of policy on domestic opinion.

237
00:17:10,816 --> 00:17:13,205
Our system doesn't seem to allow for that.

238
00:17:13,417 --> 00:17:18,126
Of course not. We take the global view.
We ask what's best for the world.

239
00:17:18,336 --> 00:17:22,046
Most ministers would ask
what will the "Daily Mail" say!

240
00:17:22,256 --> 00:17:28,446
We can't have foreign policy made by yobbos
like Fleet Street editors or backbench MPs!

241
00:17:28,615 --> 00:17:31,609
- Or Cabinet ministers.
- Or Cabinet ministers.

242
00:17:31,776 --> 00:17:35,769
We take the right decisions
and let them sort out the politics.

243
00:17:35,937 --> 00:17:40,327
Yes? Yes, come in, Mrs Whitmore.

244
00:17:41,336 --> 00:17:45,296
- Thank you.
- If the Foreign Secretary won't accept this...?

245
00:17:45,497 --> 00:17:48,091
It's a free country. He can always resign.

246
00:17:48,296 --> 00:17:52,972
How can you treat the PM like this?
After all, St George's IS a democracy!

247
00:17:53,176 --> 00:17:55,167
Not for long.

248
00:17:55,336 --> 00:18:00,251
The East Yemen are preparing to invade
in support of the Marxist guerrillas.

249
00:18:00,457 --> 00:18:02,651
- That's awful.
- Yes and no.

250
00:18:02,816 --> 00:18:07,890
Moderately bad news for the government of St
George's, but very good for the guerrillas.

251
00:18:08,095 --> 00:18:13,091
- What about the islanders?
- Bernard, you're reacting like a politician.

252
00:18:13,296 --> 00:18:15,684
- What are we going to do to help them?
- Nothing.

253
00:18:15,856 --> 00:18:21,488
- What if St George's appeals to us?
- We'll give them every support, short of help.

254
00:18:21,696 --> 00:18:26,690
- What if the Prime Minister insists?
- Then we follow the four-stage strategy.

255
00:18:26,856 --> 00:18:31,452
- What's that?
- Standard F.O. response in a time of crisis.

256
00:18:31,615 --> 00:18:34,609
In stage one we say nothing
is going to happen.

257
00:18:34,816 --> 00:18:38,126
Stage two, it may happen,
but we should do nothing.

258
00:18:38,336 --> 00:18:43,171
Stage three, maybe we should do something,
but there's nothing we CAN do.

259
00:18:43,376 --> 00:18:48,370
Stage four, maybe there was something
we could have done, but it's too late now.

260
00:18:54,655 --> 00:18:57,046
Afternoon telegrams, Prime Minister.

261
00:18:58,536 --> 00:19:00,926
Thank you, Luke.

262
00:19:02,055 --> 00:19:06,651
- Where's Bernard?
- I think he's with Sir Humphrey.

263
00:19:06,816 --> 00:19:10,696
- Troop movements in East Yemen?
- It's not significant.

264
00:19:10,896 --> 00:19:13,774
The American ambassador mentioned it.

265
00:19:13,977 --> 00:19:16,571
Really? I'm surprised he'd heard of it.

266
00:19:16,736 --> 00:19:21,251
- What are they up to?
- Just preparing a regular raid on West Yemen.

267
00:19:21,457 --> 00:19:24,448
- Nothing for us to worry about?
- Nothing at all.

268
00:19:24,655 --> 00:19:28,046
He said something about St George's, too.

269
00:19:28,256 --> 00:19:30,930
Really? Educated man.

270
00:19:32,457 --> 00:19:34,731
For an American.

271
00:19:34,937 --> 00:19:39,963
- Is there a problem there?
- No, no, no. Just the normal local squabbles.

272
00:19:40,176 --> 00:19:45,124
- He was worried about a Communist takeover.
- Americans always are.

273
00:19:45,336 --> 00:19:47,930
Luke! Have you read this?

274
00:19:48,095 --> 00:19:52,374
- We voted against Israel in the U.N. last night!
- Yes, Prime Minister.

275
00:19:52,575 --> 00:19:57,126
- But I gave express instructions to abstain.
- I think not.

276
00:19:57,336 --> 00:20:01,296
I said I felt very strongly
that we should not take sides.

277
00:20:01,497 --> 00:20:05,888
- The Foreign Secretary noted your feeling.
- But did nothing about it!

278
00:20:06,055 --> 00:20:10,926
With respect, he did. He asked our U.N.
ambassador if we should consider abstaining.

279
00:20:11,135 --> 00:20:13,126
- What did HE do?
- He said no.

280
00:20:13,336 --> 00:20:17,693
You mean the Foreign Office
can simply ignore the Prime Minister?!

281
00:20:17,896 --> 00:20:22,890
Certainly not, Prime Minister. They take full
account of you when coming to their decision,

282
00:20:23,055 --> 00:20:27,845
but events move rapidly. There were important
factors in our relationship with the Arabs

283
00:20:28,017 --> 00:20:32,966
that were not known to you when you took your
view. We couldn't get through to you in time.

284
00:20:33,176 --> 00:20:35,975
I am on the phone, you know.

285
00:20:36,135 --> 00:20:39,606
It wasn't sufficiently
important to wake you.

286
00:20:39,816 --> 00:20:42,728
It was! The White House will do its nut!

287
00:20:42,937 --> 00:20:48,249
You could be telephoned before every U.N.
vote, but there are two or three a night.

288
00:20:48,457 --> 00:20:53,053
- What can I do to reverse this?
- Nothing. That would be most embarrassing.

289
00:20:53,216 --> 00:20:55,684
Once stated, policy can't be retracted.

290
00:20:56,696 --> 00:21:01,725
- I'd like to talk to the Israeli ambassador.
- I think not, Prime Minister.

291
00:21:02,937 --> 00:21:05,734
I want to talk to the Israeli ambassador.

292
00:21:05,937 --> 00:21:08,450
If I may say so, it would be rather unwise.

293
00:21:08,655 --> 00:21:14,015
Luke! Can you hear me? Watch my lips!
I want to talk to the Israeli ambassador!

294
00:21:15,055 --> 00:21:17,446
Of course, Prime Minister,
if that is your wish.

295
00:21:17,615 --> 00:21:21,655
I will contact the Foreign Secretary
and then telephone...

296
00:21:21,856 --> 00:21:24,847
I don't want the Foreign Secretary!

297
00:21:25,055 --> 00:21:30,528
Prime Minister, it would be most improper to
see him without the Foreign Secretary present.

298
00:21:30,736 --> 00:21:34,968
- Why? What do you think I want to talk about?
- Presumably the vote at the U.N.

299
00:21:35,176 --> 00:21:38,771
- That would be most improper.
- Oh.

300
00:21:38,977 --> 00:21:43,573
My daughter Lucy wishes to spend
her next long vacation on a kibbutz.

301
00:21:43,736 --> 00:21:47,968
Or I should say, as she's at the
University of Sussex, another kibbutz.

302
00:21:48,176 --> 00:21:51,771
- I see.
- The ambassador and I were at the LSE.

303
00:21:51,937 --> 00:21:57,805
I thought I'd invite him to the flat tonight for
a drink. 6 p.m. See to it, would you, Luke?

304
00:21:58,017 --> 00:22:02,008
- Yes, Prime Minister.
- Thank you.

305
00:22:08,216 --> 00:22:13,006
- Don't be upset, Jim. It happens all the time.
- I told them to abstain.

306
00:22:13,216 --> 00:22:18,006
It's well known that in the British Foreign
Office an instruction from the Prime Minister

307
00:22:18,176 --> 00:22:22,966
becomes a request from the Foreign Secretary,
a recommendation from the Minister of State

308
00:22:23,135 --> 00:22:27,254
and, finally, a suggestion to the
ambassador. If it ever gets that far.

309
00:22:27,457 --> 00:22:29,845
Thank you. L'Chaim.

310
00:22:30,017 --> 00:22:31,814
Cheers.

311
00:22:31,977 --> 00:22:35,969
Well, Jim, what are you going to do
about St George's?

312
00:22:36,135 --> 00:22:39,334
- You know about that?
- Obviously.

313
00:22:39,497 --> 00:22:44,093
- Not a serious problem, is it?
- Your information must be better than mine.

314
00:22:44,296 --> 00:22:46,856
Mine comes from the Foreign Office.

315
00:22:48,055 --> 00:22:50,251
Israeli Intelligence says

316
00:22:50,417 --> 00:22:54,613
that East Yemen are going to invade
St George's Island in the next few days.

317
00:22:54,816 --> 00:22:56,613
What?!

318
00:22:56,776 --> 00:23:01,566
- So that's the connection.
- Your Foreign Office agreed with East Yemen

319
00:23:01,736 --> 00:23:06,332
that they'll make strong diplomatic
representations, but do nothing.

320
00:23:06,497 --> 00:23:11,207
- In return, you keep your airport contract.
- There will be uproar!

321
00:23:11,417 --> 00:23:13,408
That's only the start.

322
00:23:13,575 --> 00:23:18,526
I happen to know that the Americans are going
to support the present St George's government.

323
00:23:18,736 --> 00:23:22,809
- In the U.N...?
- No, in battle. On St George's Island.

324
00:23:23,017 --> 00:23:26,372
An airborne division and the Seventh Fleet.

325
00:23:26,575 --> 00:23:29,135
Invading a Commonwealth country?!

326
00:23:29,296 --> 00:23:33,289
The Palace will hit the roof.
And I'll look ridiculous.

327
00:23:33,457 --> 00:23:36,731
- Why didn't they tell me?
- They don't trust you.

328
00:23:36,937 --> 00:23:42,408
- Because you trust the Foreign Office.
- Oh, I see. What can I do about it?

329
00:23:42,615 --> 00:23:47,211
Jim, you have an airborne battalion
on standby in Germany

330
00:23:47,376 --> 00:23:49,765
that is not now needed
for the NATO exercise.

331
00:23:49,977 --> 00:23:52,968
- How do you know?
- I know.

332
00:23:53,655 --> 00:23:58,890
Now, if you were to send it to St George's
Island, it would frighten off East Yemen.

333
00:23:59,095 --> 00:24:01,325
They'd never dare invade.

334
00:24:01,536 --> 00:24:05,892
Of course, it's not for the Israeli ambassador
to advise the British Prime Minister.

335
00:24:06,095 --> 00:24:08,690
And he wouldn't take your advice anyway.

336
00:24:12,055 --> 00:24:16,448
Get me the Foreign Secretary
and the Defence Secretary.

337
00:24:16,615 --> 00:24:21,405
I wonder why the Foreign Office didn't
cover themselves. Maybe they did.

338
00:24:21,575 --> 00:24:26,365
They gave me several boxes tonight.
I've been through them all except this one.

339
00:24:26,536 --> 00:24:29,131
I wonder if this could be it.

340
00:24:29,296 --> 00:24:32,287
"Northern Indian Ocean Situation Report".

341
00:24:33,296 --> 00:24:35,684
It's 138 pages. It must be it.

342
00:24:38,417 --> 00:24:40,805
Hello? Yes, Ronnie.

343
00:24:40,977 --> 00:24:45,970
I want the president of St George's Island to
invite Britain to send an airborne battalion

344
00:24:46,135 --> 00:24:48,526
on a goodwill visit.

345
00:24:48,696 --> 00:24:51,084
No, just a friendly gesture. Goodwill.

346
00:24:51,256 --> 00:24:54,248
Yes, at once, please. Thank you.

347
00:24:55,256 --> 00:25:00,249
He seemed to think that 800 paratroopers was
an awful lot to send on a goodwill visit.

348
00:25:02,896 --> 00:25:05,968
No, it's just an awful lot of goodwill.

349
00:25:08,736 --> 00:25:13,526
Oh, yes, Paul. You know you have an
airborne battalion on standby in Germany?

350
00:25:13,696 --> 00:25:16,084
Never mind how I know.

351
00:25:16,256 --> 00:25:21,249
Well, since it's not being used, I want them
to fly straight off to St George's Island.

352
00:25:21,417 --> 00:25:24,213
Sort of between Africa and India.

353
00:25:25,856 --> 00:25:28,528
A goodwill visit. Just showing the flag.

354
00:25:29,536 --> 00:25:34,132
They have been invited. Yes.
Leave in... six hours.

355
00:25:35,135 --> 00:25:37,729
Yes, an instant goodwill visit.

356
00:25:37,896 --> 00:25:40,285
Tell your press office
to announce it at once.

357
00:25:40,457 --> 00:25:43,448
No, no, leave me out of it.
A routine visit.

358
00:25:43,615 --> 00:25:46,414
All right - a routine surprise visit.

359
00:25:47,856 --> 00:25:52,247
Well, say they were invited earlier,
but the NATO exercise got in the way.

360
00:25:52,417 --> 00:25:57,410
Now they're not needed, they're going.
All right. Nobody knows it's not true.

361
00:25:57,575 --> 00:26:00,171
Press statements aren't
delivered under oath.

362
00:26:02,055 --> 00:26:04,446
They'll be off at midnight.

363
00:26:05,977 --> 00:26:10,129
- There's an airborne battalion in the air.
- Sounds like the right place for it!

364
00:26:12,457 --> 00:26:17,928
- I gather it's on its way to St George's.
- Yes, it should land in two hours' time.

365
00:26:18,135 --> 00:26:22,731
- Isn't this all rather sudden?
- I had a sudden friendly impulse.

366
00:26:22,896 --> 00:26:27,651
- I wanted to spread a little goodwill.
- There's not much goodwill in the F.O.

367
00:26:27,856 --> 00:26:31,850
- Really? Why not?
- It might be construed as provocative,

368
00:26:32,017 --> 00:26:37,455
sending a fully-armed airborne battalion
like that into an explosive situation.

369
00:26:37,655 --> 00:26:42,251
- I thought there was no problem there.
- Eh... well, yes, indeed.

370
00:26:42,417 --> 00:26:46,249
There is no problem, but
it's explosive potentially.

371
00:26:46,457 --> 00:26:51,053
- Why?
- Moving troops is always potentially explosive.

372
00:26:51,256 --> 00:26:55,646
We move them around Salisbury Plain.
Is that potentially explosive?

373
00:26:55,856 --> 00:26:58,575
There are a lot of unexploded shells on...

374
00:26:59,776 --> 00:27:05,213
Thank you, Bernard. Do tell me why the
Foreign Office is worried. I'm fascinated.

375
00:27:05,417 --> 00:27:10,046
- It's a sensitive part of the world. Unstable.
- They always tell me how stable it is.

376
00:27:10,256 --> 00:27:13,133
Well, yes, indeed it is... very,

377
00:27:13,296 --> 00:27:16,892
but it's a sort of...
unstable sort of stability.

378
00:27:18,615 --> 00:27:21,414
- Excuse me, Sir Humphrey.
- Yes, Luke.

379
00:27:21,575 --> 00:27:26,571
- Foreign Office telegrams.
- Thank you, Luke. Oh, gosh. Rather a lot.

380
00:27:26,736 --> 00:27:31,571
Yes, the somewhat unorthodox visit
to St George's has stirred things up.

381
00:27:31,776 --> 00:27:35,769
Ah, I see East Yemen
is moving its troops back to base.

382
00:27:35,977 --> 00:27:40,367
- Yes, Prime Minister.
- Decided not to invade West Yemen?

383
00:27:40,575 --> 00:27:43,568
- So it seems.
- Oh, this is nice.

384
00:27:43,736 --> 00:27:47,332
The Americans are delighted by our visit.
That's good.

385
00:27:47,497 --> 00:27:50,249
Excellent, Prime Minister.

386
00:27:50,457 --> 00:27:55,211
- They're ready if we want reinforcements.
- Reinforcements of what?

387
00:27:55,417 --> 00:27:58,374
Reinforcements of goodwill, Humphrey.

388
00:28:00,296 --> 00:28:05,131
May I inquire where the impulse
for this little escapade came from?

389
00:28:05,336 --> 00:28:09,124
- Of course you may. It came from Luke.
- Luke?!

390
00:28:09,296 --> 00:28:11,251
From me?

391
00:28:11,457 --> 00:28:16,325
It was you who put together that masterly
Northern Indian Ocean Situation Report?

392
00:28:16,536 --> 00:28:18,926
Yes, but it argued for not doing anything.

393
00:28:19,135 --> 00:28:22,129
- Come off it, Luke. You can't fool me.
- What?!

394
00:28:22,336 --> 00:28:24,850
I can read between the lines.

395
00:28:25,017 --> 00:28:29,008
Some politicians have a
feeling for foreign affairs.

396
00:28:29,176 --> 00:28:32,613
I knew you meant St
George's needed support.

397
00:28:32,816 --> 00:28:35,410
Oh, yes. Well, no, actually.

398
00:28:36,417 --> 00:28:39,055
Only in one paragraph on page 107.

399
00:28:39,256 --> 00:28:41,644
It was enough. I can take the hint.

400
00:28:41,816 --> 00:28:47,367
I'm giving you full credit. I told the Foreign
Secretary it was your warning sparked it off.

401
00:28:47,575 --> 00:28:51,363
No, no, it wasn't! You haven't?!

402
00:28:51,575 --> 00:28:56,696
And I don't think I'm giving away any secrets
when I say you are going to be rewarded.

403
00:28:56,896 --> 00:29:02,095
- Rewarded?
- Ambassador at a very important embassy.

404
00:29:02,296 --> 00:29:04,809
- Which embassy?
- Tel Aviv.

405
00:29:05,017 --> 00:29:09,566
Oh, my God! You can't send me to Israel.
Think about my career.

406
00:29:09,776 --> 00:29:12,495
Don't be absurd. It's an honour. Promotion.

407
00:29:12,696 --> 00:29:16,211
They won't want me. I'm on the Arab side.

408
00:29:17,457 --> 00:29:21,164
- I thought you were on OUR side.
- Of course, yes, but...

409
00:29:21,376 --> 00:29:26,847
We need somebody like you in Tel Aviv
to explain why we always vote against them.

410
00:29:27,017 --> 00:29:29,814
Don't we, Humphrey?

411
00:29:29,977 --> 00:29:32,171
Yes, Prime Minister.

412
00:29:34,837 --> 00:29:42,837
http://episode-heaven.rt.hu

